One issue: the "版本源失败时已有下载任务仍保留" test asserts `/42%/`. `dlTaskHtml` renders `<span class="dl-task-status">${esc(t.statusLabel || '')} ${t.progress ? (t.progress + '%') : ''}</span>` → '下载中 42%' ✓.

Another: `data-empty="tasks"` — the empty state has `style="padding:32px" data-empty="tasks"`. My assertion `doesNotMatch(second, /data-empty="tasks"/)` in the retained-tasks test ✓ and the tasks-error test asserts `doesNotMatch(html, /暂无下载任务/)` — but `dl.noTasks` is only rendered in the ready+empty branch, so in the error branch it's absent ✓.

Hmm, wait: in the tasks-error test the versions succeed → vlist has a row. The version row for `1.20.1` renders `data-dlver="1.20.1"`. My assertion `/1\.20\.1/` ✓.

Now — `_dlTasks` id: `class="list-grid download-tasks" id="dlTasks"` ✓.

Next: `reloadDownloadsView(el, triggerBtn)` — `triggerBtn.disabled = true` then in the finally we don't reset it. If the view is NOT re-rendered (page switched away), the button stays disabled forever. Consider: user clicks retry, switches page, request completes → `return false` inside try → finally sets dlReloadInFlight=false, but the OLD button stays disabled. When the user returns to the downloads page, `goTo('downloads')` → if `el.dataset.rendered` is already '1', goTo skips re-render (it only re-renders content pages). So the user could come back to a page with a permanently disabled retry button. That's a real (if minor) defect.

Fix: in the early-return paths, restore `triggerBtn.disabled = false`. Better: use a `finally` that resets the button when we did NOT re-render. Simplest: track a `rendered` flag:

```js
async function reloadDownloadsView(el, triggerBtn) {
	if (dlReloadInFlight) return false;
	dlReloadInFlight = true;
	if (triggerBtn && typeof triggerBtn === 'object') triggerBtn.disabled = true;
	let rendered = false;
	try {
		const html = await renderDownloads();
		if (!html) return false;
		if (currentView !== 'downloads') return false;
		const view = document.getElementById('view-downloads') || el;
		if (!view) return false;
		view.innerHTML = html;
		bindDownloads(view);
		rendered = true;
		return true;
	} finally {
		dlReloadInFlight = false;
		// 未重渲染（页面已切走 / 代际过期）时把按钮恢复可用，避免留下永久禁用的按钮。
		if (!rendered && triggerBtn && typeof triggerBtn === 'object' && triggerBtn.isConnected !== false) {
			triggerBtn.disabled = false;
		}
	}
}
```
Hmm — `triggerBtn.isConnected !== false`: in a real DOM, after re-render the old button is disconnected; but we only reach here when NOT rendered, so the button is still connected (or in tests, `_connected`). Let me keep it simple: `if (!rendered && triggerBtn) triggerBtn.disabled = false;`.

Wait — there's an important subtlety with my test "页面已切走时，旧请求结束不写 DOM": it asserts the DOM string is unchanged. Restoring `disabled = false` doesn't change innerHTML ✓. Good.

But the concurrency test: after 3 clicks, the first reload is still pending (never resolves) so the finally never runs → the button stays disabled ✓. My assertion `btn.disabled === true` holds ✓.

Let me apply this fix.

Also for the filter-button path, `reloadDownloadsView(el)` has no triggerBtn → no-op ✓.

Another consideration: `bindDownloads` is called on `view` after re-render; the old listeners on the OLD child nodes are gone ✓. But note `bindDownloads(el)` uses `el.querySelectorAll` — the new children ✓.

One more: in the `data-dlver` click handler, on success we call `reloadDownloadsView(el)`; if a retry is in flight, this silently no-ops (returns false) — the queued download's UI won't refresh. Acceptable.

Let me now apply the fix.

